WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
User feedback: useful, biased, never the whole story.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:17.496
Explicit feedback (thumbs up and down, ratings, a “report” button) is cheap to collect and valuable for finding failures, but it’s a biased sample: most users never click, and the ones who do skew toward strong reactions.

3
00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:29.893
Implicit signals often tell you more: the user regenerates the answer, edits a drafted reply heavily before sending, copies the answer, abandons the session, or escalates to a human.

4
00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:32.648
Use feedback two ways, and keep them apart.

5
00:00:32.928 --> 00:00:41.845
As discovery, every thumbs-down goes into the review queue with its trace id attached, which is why the trace id belongs on every feedback event.

6
00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:58.189
As measurement, chart feedback rates over time per route and version, but never treat the thumbs-down rate as your quality estimate: the random sample with a calibrated judge is your measurement, and feedback is a second, biased signal that should move in the same direction.

7
00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:02.651
When the two disagree, investigate; that disagreement is information.
